at once (false discovery rate q = 0.05); most of them repeat the main trend month by month. Findings marked “follow-up check” come from these checks."},"deeper_checks":null,"provenance":{"dataset":"NASA AIRS (Aqua satellite) · TotH2OVap","version":"7.0","doi":"10.5067/UBENJB9D3T2H","landing_page":"https://doi.org/10.5067/UBENJB9D3T2H","accessed":"2026-11-15","processing":["one OPeNDAP DAP4 subset per monthly granule: SurfAirTemp_A, SurfAirTemp_D, TotH2OVap_A, TotH2OVap_D (no other fields)","masked fill value -9999","country value = mean of the 2° map squares that overlap the country, weighted by the overlapping area (mainland and islands within about 1,000 km)","monthly series 2003-01 to 2025-12 (complete years)","anomalies from the calendar-month normal; Sen's slope, Mann-Kendall (Yue-Wang), OLS with effective sample size (trend_stats.py)"],"synthetic":false,"caveat":"Data: NASA AIRS infrared sounder on the Aqua satellite, 1° squares, average of the ~1:30 am and ~1:30 pm passes; a satellite retrieval, not a weather model (MERRA-2 does use AIRS measurements, so the two are not fully independent) (averaged into 2° squares; coastal squares include some sea). The trend is a straight line through 2003–2025; it shows what changed, not why. The record is 23 years long, shorter than the 30 years usually used to describe a climate. AIRS sees mostly clear and partly cloudy skies, and its global water-vapour trend is much smaller than MERRA-2's (about +0.1 vs +0.5 kg/m² per decade, 2003–2025); treat this trend as not yet confirmed."}}